What determines GPU speedup
---------------------------

.. grid:: 1 1 3 3
   :gutter: 2

   .. grid-item-card:: Compute intensity

      GPU acceleration has more room to pay off when estimator work dominates
      dispatch and data-conversion overhead. Compute-heavy fitting, clustering,
      nearest-neighbor search, forests, and high-rank decomposition are often
      strong candidates.

   .. grid-item-card:: Operation and shape

      Rows alone do not determine performance. Feature count, retained
      components, and the exact operation also matter. Evaluate fit, predict,
      and transform separately; these benchmark points are not a controlled
      scaling curve.

   .. grid-item-card:: End-to-end workflow

      Realized application performance also depends on data location,
      conversions, batching, and repeated calls. Use these results to identify
      promising candidates, then measure the production path.

Methodology and reproducibility
-------------------------------

These benchmarks compare scikit-learn CPU execution with ``cuml.accel`` on
NVIDIA RTX Pro 6000 Blackwell across five workload shapes. Each isolated case
used one warmup, the median of three measured repetitions,
operation-appropriate correctness validation, and a complete-case timeout.

.. dropdown:: Test system, validation, and timing policy

   **System.** @@GPU_NAME@@ (@@GPU_MEMORY_GB@@ GB), @@CPU_NAME@@, and
   @@SYSTEM_MEMORY_GB@@ GB of system memory.

   **Timing.** One warmup followed by three measured repetitions; tables use
   the median end-to-end wall time. Speedup is CPU median wall time divided by
   accelerated median wall time.

   **Scale and timeout policy.** Fixed decimal-byte small and medium shapes
   plus operation-specific large shapes. Each backend ran every case in a
   separate worker with a @@DEFAULT_TIMEOUT_MIN@@-minute wall-clock limit,
   extended to @@LARGE_TIMEOUT_MIN@@ minutes for large workloads other than
   PCA ``fit_transform``. The limit covered process startup, data preparation,
   estimator setup, one warmup, three measured repetitions, and correctness
   validation. The results retain @@CPU_TIMEOUTS@@ CPU-only, @@GPU_TIMEOUTS@@
   GPU-only, and @@BOTH_TIMEOUTS@@ both-side timeouts.

   **Execution and correctness.** Successful accelerated measurements were
   instrumented to verify GPU-only execution. The benchmark runner applied
   each case's operation-appropriate parity check. A timeout means validation
   could not complete.

   **Packages.** @@PACKAGES@@.

   **Coverage and scope.** All @@CASES@@ cases are included, including all
   @@UNAVAILABLE@@ unavailable comparisons and every completed result below
   1×. These measurements describe the tested cases on this system; results
   for other workloads and systems will vary. PCA's operation-specific
   ``large`` fit-transform uses 5,000 rows, 2,048 features, and 1,024 retained
   components.

